WEBVTT

00:00.086 --> 00:01.490
- [Voiceover] So I have a circle here,

00:01.490 --> 00:05.345
with a center at point O and
let's pick an arbitrary point

00:05.345 --> 00:07.109
that sits outside of the circle.

00:07.109 --> 00:09.524
So let me just pick this
point right over here.

00:09.524 --> 00:11.010
Point A.

00:11.010 --> 00:13.726
And if I have an arbitrary
point outside of the circle,

00:13.726 --> 00:16.083
I can actually draw two
different tangent lines

00:16.083 --> 00:19.949
that contain A, that are
tangent to this circle.

00:19.949 --> 00:21.272
Let me draw them.

00:21.272 --> 00:24.835
So one of them would look like this.

00:24.835 --> 00:27.464
Let me just start right over here.

00:27.464 --> 00:29.208
So I make a tangent to the circle.

00:29.208 --> 00:31.604
So it could look like that.

00:31.604 --> 00:35.841
And then the other one
would look like this,

00:35.841 --> 00:38.105
would look like,

00:38.105 --> 00:39.997
would look like that.

00:39.997 --> 00:41.182
Now let's say that the point

00:41.182 --> 00:44.362
that the tangent lines
intersect the circle,

00:44.362 --> 00:49.238
let's say this point right
over here, is point B,

00:49.238 --> 00:53.522
and this point right over here is point C.

00:53.522 --> 00:55.716
This is point C. Right over here.

00:55.716 --> 00:57.283
What I want to prove

00:57.283 --> 01:01.962
What I want to prove
is that the segment AB

01:01.962 --> 01:04.414
is congruent to the segment AC,

01:04.414 --> 01:07.825
or another way of thinking
about it, I want to prove,

01:07.825 --> 01:10.588
let me do this in a new color
that I haven't used yet,

01:10.588 --> 01:15.588
I want to prove that this
segment, right over here,

01:16.590 --> 01:20.084
is congruent to this segment...

01:20.084 --> 01:23.683
is congruent to that segment.

01:23.683 --> 01:25.888
I encourage you to pause the video

01:25.888 --> 01:29.661
and try to work it out on your own

01:29.661 --> 01:32.006
before I work through it.

01:32.006 --> 01:34.467
Alright, now let's try to
work through this together.

01:34.467 --> 01:35.490
And to work through this together,

01:35.490 --> 01:38.394
I'm going to actually
set up two triangles.

01:38.394 --> 01:39.532
Two triangles.

01:39.532 --> 01:40.735
And they're going to be right triangles

01:40.735 --> 01:41.916
as we'll see in a second.

01:41.916 --> 01:46.246
So let me draw some lines
here to set up our triangles.

01:46.246 --> 01:47.907
So I'm going to draw one line,

01:47.907 --> 01:52.907
just like that, and then we draw that.

01:53.241 --> 01:56.583
And then, let me draw that.

01:56.583 --> 01:59.071
Now what do we know

01:59.071 --> 02:00.472
about these triangles?

02:00.472 --> 02:01.435
Well, as I mentioned,

02:01.435 --> 02:02.979
we're going to be dealing
with right triangles.

02:02.979 --> 02:04.012
How do I know that?

02:04.012 --> 02:05.115
Well, in a previous video, we saw that

02:05.115 --> 02:09.039
if we have a radius
intersecting a tangent line,

02:09.039 --> 02:11.999
that they intersect at right angles.

02:11.999 --> 02:13.636
We've proven this.

02:13.636 --> 02:15.253
So that's a radius. That's a tangent line.

02:15.253 --> 02:16.949
They're going to intersect
at a right angle.

02:16.949 --> 02:21.906
So radius, tangent line, they
intersect at a right angle.

02:21.906 --> 02:26.341
We also know, since OB
and OC are both radii,

02:26.341 --> 02:28.500
that they're both the length
of the radius of the circle.

02:28.500 --> 02:30.705
So this side, right over here,

02:30.705 --> 02:32.598
let me do some new colors here,

02:32.598 --> 02:36.278
so this side is going to
be congruent to that side.

02:36.278 --> 02:39.586
And you can see that the
hypotenuse of both circles

02:39.586 --> 02:42.582
is the same side, side OA,

02:42.582 --> 02:44.848
so of course, it is equal to itself.

02:44.848 --> 02:46.367
This is equal to itself.

02:46.367 --> 02:51.185
So we see triangle ABO and triangle ACO,

02:51.185 --> 02:55.108
they're both right triangles
that have two sides in common,

02:55.108 --> 02:56.943
in particular, they both have a hypotenuse

02:56.943 --> 02:57.999
that are equal to each other

02:57.999 --> 03:00.379
and they both have a base, or a leg.

03:00.379 --> 03:03.200
And we know from Hypotenuse-Leg Congruence

03:03.200 --> 03:04.767
that if you have two right triangles,

03:04.767 --> 03:07.333
where the hypotenuses are equal,

03:07.333 --> 03:08.847
and you have a leg that are equal,

03:08.847 --> 03:12.307
then the both triangles
are going to be congruent.

03:12.307 --> 03:17.008
So triangle ABO is
congruent to triangle ACO.

03:17.008 --> 03:18.924
And in that proof where
we prove it, as well,

03:18.924 --> 03:20.897
the Pythagorean theorem tells us

03:20.897 --> 03:22.047
if you know two sides of a right triangle

03:22.047 --> 03:24.380
that determines what the third side is.

03:24.380 --> 03:26.981
So the third side, so length of AB

03:26.981 --> 03:29.418
is going to be the same
thing as the length of AC.

03:29.418 --> 03:30.986
Once again, it just comes out of,

03:30.986 --> 03:33.284
these are both right
triangles, if two sides,

03:33.284 --> 03:35.641
if two corresponding sides
of these two right triangles

03:35.641 --> 03:37.220
are congruent, then the third side has to,

03:37.220 --> 03:39.182
that comes straight from
the Pythagorean theorem.

03:39.182 --> 03:40.203
And there you go.

03:40.203 --> 03:42.722
We hopefully feel good about the fact that

03:42.722 --> 03:45.137
AB is going to be congruent to AC.

03:45.137 --> 03:45.984
Or another way to think about it,

03:45.984 --> 03:47.761
if I take a point outside of a circle,

03:47.761 --> 03:50.698
and I construct segments that
are tangent to the circle,

03:50.698 --> 03:52.323
that those two segments are going to be

03:52.323 --> 03:54.767
congruent to each other.
